
  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the app decides [3mwhich[0m environment it's in at the moment the page loads. The settings module holds the
  values either way; what differs is what you physically do to point at staging instead of prod — and that choice
  determines whether this repo stays a zero-build static page or grows a build step.

  - [1mRuntime detection from the URL[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m maps [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m to an endpoint. Switching environments is
    just visiting the other host; nothing to remember at deploy time. Cost: every environment's URL ships in the
    source, visible to anyone who views it. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mManual constant edit[0m — one [38;5;153mconst ENV = "production"[39m line at the top of [38;5;153msettings.js[39m that you change by hand. Dead
    simple, no new machinery. Cost: it's a code edit before each deploy, and forgetting it is the classic way prod
    traffic lands on staging. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — a real build step substitutes the endpoint per environment. The robust answer for a grown-up
    app. Cost: introduces a toolchain, a [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m script, and a generated artifact into a repo that currently has
    none. Expensive to undo; it changes how the project is run and deployed from here on.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime detection from the URL.[0m It matches the zero-build static page you actually have, makes
  switching environments a property of where the app is deployed rather than a step someone has to remember, and it
  doesn't foreclose build-time injection later — the settings module stays the single seam either way. The exposed URLs
  aren't a real concern here since a public API endpoint is visible in network traffic regardless.
